-- #1620 CONTROL — main.mdk with the SECOND interface and its impl deleted, and
-- nothing else changed. ONE dimension flips: whether a second interface declares
-- the same method name.
--
-- It is graded with the SAME verb as the pin (`build-run`), so it exercises the
-- whole path — front end, emitter, clang, and the executed binary — that the pin
-- claims delivers a wrong value only under the collision. A red here means
-- single-interface dispatch, the emitter or clang broke, not that #1620 is fixed,
-- and the harness says CONTROL-BROKE rather than DRAINED.
--
-- Per #1144 a control is graded on its EXIT CODE ONLY, so it ASSERTS "alpha" and
-- panics otherwise rather than printing it — a control that merely printed would
-- exit 0 either way and could never fail. PROVEN ABLE TO FAIL: changing the
-- comparand to a value the program cannot produce, "nope", makes the built
-- binary exit 1 with `runtime error [E-PANIC]: control regressed: ...`
-- (measured 2026-08-14, then reverted).
interface Alpha a where
  ping : a -> String

data T = T

impl Alpha T where
  ping _ = "alpha"

main =
  if ping T == "alpha"
    then println "control-ok:alpha"
    else panic "control regressed: a lone interface's method no longer delivers its own impl body"
